# Recursive Design Flow (`>scope`)

`>scope: <objective>` runs a recursive, phase-ordered elicitation that turns a
broad or ambiguous objective into a **decision-complete analysis**. It is the
middle rung of the analysis ladder: `>draft` captures raw notes without asking,
`>scope` elicits with batched questions, `>analysis` authors or extends a full
analysis — all three converge on a `status: draft` analysis.

The flow is agent-side and conversational: there is no `sdt scope` CLI, no
questionnaire engine and no state machine. The only artifact is the analysis
file. Use it for an objective too broad or ambiguous to write straight into an
analysis; a well-scoped objective goes directly to `>analysis`.

## Invocation

- Trigger: `>scope`.
- Payload: the objective to scope, per the shared grammar in
  `context/commands/index.md` — everything after the first `:`, verbatim, to
  the end of the turn. This contract does not restate the grammar.
- With no payload, ask for the objective in **one** question, then proceed with
  the answer. Never infer it from the working context.
- **The payload narrows the objective; it never authorises the write.** Confirm
  once — `scope <objective> into a draft analysis?` — before creating the
  `status: draft` analysis, then treat that confirmation as covering the whole
  flow (its appends and the final `status` flip). The plan gate is untouched:
  this flow ends at the analysis, and `>plan` is a separate command needing its
  own approval.

## The flow (phases are ordered; do not run ahead)

0. **Constitution** — only if the user has already fixed non-negotiables (stack,
   standards, explicit exclusions): capture them in one confirmation and treat
   them as fixed. If nothing is fixed, skip the phase without asking.
1. **Specify (tech-free)** — beneficiary, success criterion, what concretely
   changes. No framework, language or component name may appear here. If the
   user already said it, validate it in one line and move on.
2. **Clarify (recursive)** — behaviour and rules. Multiple choice when the
   alternatives are enumerable, prose when a description is needed, and a
   concrete example ("a real case: X happens — what must the system do?")
   whenever the subject is a behaviour rule. After each answer, check whether it
   opened new sub-questions.
3. **Plan (technical)** — architecture, stack, contracts, persistence,
   errors/edge cases, security, performance, deploy/operations, testing and
   acceptance. The stack choice runs through the library-first evaluation of
   HARD RULE 8 (web search + shortlist + user approval), then records the chosen
   library, not just the category. Irrelevant areas are skipped **without
   asking**.
4. **Close** — run the decomposition gate (below), then finalise the analysis:
   `summary` written, `## Constraints`, `## Behaviour decisions`,
   `## Design decisions` and `## Assumptions & Unknowns` completed, residual
   points registered in `context/questions/`, `updated` refreshed with
   `sdt context touch`, and `status` flipped to `active` with
   `sdt context status set`. Then stop.

## Elicitation rhythm

- Batch up to **three independent** choices per turn; a batchable choice has
  enumerable alternatives.
- Ask **alone** anything whose answer changes the next question.
- This deliberately overrides the reference skill's "one question per turn": do
  not "fix" it back.

## Non-pedantry (operational)

A question is worth asking only when **a different answer would change the
implementation outcome**. Everything else gets a default, taken by the agent and
declared in `## Assumptions & Unknowns`.

- **Ask**: choices that look reversible but are expensive to reverse (data model,
  public contract, dependency); behaviour rules whose wrong answer wastes a
  phase; anything the exit test below flags.
- **Do not ask**: naming, file layout, formatting, values with an obvious
  default, and anything the user already answered in the objective or in an
  active analysis (integrate it — HARD RULE 1).

## Termination (the exit test)

The flow ends when, for every applicable area, exactly one state holds:

- **decided** — the answer is recorded with its source (user answer, repo
  evidence, library documentation);
- **assumed** — a declared assumption with a confidence level and a stated
  default;
- **deferred** — registered in `context/questions/` with the options and a
  suggested default, so the flow closes without an open point in the analysis.

and the **summary test** holds: *an implementer starting now would not have to
guess either what to build or how, and every choice they would have to make is
written down.* The user may close at any time ("enough", "wrap up"); the areas
left open are recorded as assumptions or questions — **never** as an "Open
points" section.

## Write timing and resumability

- Create the analysis on the **first turn**:
  `sdt context new --type analysis --title "<objective>" --status draft
  --objective <slug> --summary "<one line>"`; append phase by phase.
- Keep a `## Scope flow` block in the body with `state: <phase>` and the
  coverage states, so a new session resumes instead of restarting.
- `>scope: <same objective>` with an existing draft of that objective
  **continues** it (after confirming with the user); it never creates a sibling.
- An abandoned draft stays `draft` (the honest state); `sdt context status`
  counts it. Never archive it to hide it.

## Port mapping (reference → analysis contract)

| Reference (v2) | Analysis destination |
|---|---|
| What and why (Specify) | `## Problem statement` (+ `## Current state` for facts) |
| Non-negotiable constraints (Constitution) | `## Constraints` (optional) |
| Behaviour decisions (Clarify) | `## Behaviour decisions` (optional) |
| Technical decisions (Plan) | `## Design decisions` (optional), referenced by the plan |
| Assumptions | `## Assumptions & Unknowns` (mandatory) |
| Open / to be defined | **not in the analysis** → `context/questions/` |
| output file path | `context/analysis/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug>.md` (`draft` → `active`) |

## Decomposition gate

If the objective turns out to be broad — several domains, more than one
recommendation, or more than one plan — do **not** write one giant analysis:
propose the split (sibling slugs, shared `objective`, umbrella document) in
**one** question and wait (**R1**). Record the options found along the way as
postponed options per **R3**. Both rules are in
`context/instructions/analysis.md`.

## Rules

- Compose, do not restate, HARD RULES 1, 6, 7 and 8 (intent gate, frontmatter +
  `summary`, style/architecture agreed a priori, library-first).
- No open points in the analysis: residuals go to `context/questions/`, never an
  "Open points" section.
- The flow produces the analysis only; the plan is `>plan` and needs its own
  approval.
- All file mutations go through the CLI (`sdt context new`, `sdt context status
  set`, `sdt context touch`, `sdt context check`) — never hand-write state.
